{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query applies a couple of optimizations automatically to ensure that your components only re-render when they actually need to. This is done by the following means:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"structural-sharing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"structural sharing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query uses a technique called \"structural sharing\" to ensure that as many references as possible will be kept intact between re-renders. If data is fetched over the network, usually, you'll get a completely new reference by json parsing the response. However, Preact Query will keep the original reference if "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nothing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" changed in the data. If a subset changed, Preact Query will keep the unchanged parts and only replace the changed parts."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: This optimization only works if the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" returns JSON compatible data. You can turn it off by setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuralSharing: false"},{"type":"text","value":" globally or on a per-query basis, or you can implement your own structural sharing by passing a function to it."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"referential-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"referential identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The top level object returned from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useMutation"},{"type":"text","value":" and the Array returned from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQueries"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not referentially stable"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It will be a new reference on every render. However, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" properties returned from these hooks will be as stable as possible."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tracked-properties","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tracked properties"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query will only trigger a re-render if one of the properties returned from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" is actually \"used\". This is done by using "},{"type":"link","href":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Proxy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proxy object"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This avoids a lot of unnecessary re-renders, e.g. because properties like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isStale"},{"type":"text","value":" might change often, but are not used in the component."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can customize this feature by setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"notifyOnChangeProps"},{"type":"text","value":" manually globally or on a per-query basis. If you want to turn that feature off, you can set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"notifyOnChangeProps: 'all'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: The get trap of a proxy is invoked by accessing a property, either via destructuring or by accessing it directly. If you use object rest destructuring, you will disable this optimization. We have a "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../eslint/no-rest-destructuring.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"lint rule"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to guard against this pitfall."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"select","children":[{"type":"text","value":"select"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" option to select a subset of the data that your component should subscribe to. This is useful for highly optimized data transformations or to avoid unnecessary re-renders."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const useTodos = (select) => {\n  return useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    select,\n  })\n}\n\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos((data) => data.length)\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A component using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTodoCount"},{"type":"text","value":" custom hook will only re-render if the length of the todos changes. It will "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" re-render if e.g. the name of a todo changed."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" operates on successfully cached data and is not the appropriate place to throw errors. The source of truth for errors is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function that returns an error results in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" being "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" being "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". We recommend handling errors in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" if you wish to have a query fail on incorrect data, or outside of the query hook if you have an error case not related to caching."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"memoization","children":[{"type":"text","value":"memoization"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function will only re-run if:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function itself changed referentially"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" changed"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This means that an inlined "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function, as shown above, will run on every render. To avoid this, you can wrap the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCallback"},{"type":"text","value":", or extract it to a stable function reference if it doesn't have any dependencies:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// wrapped in useCallback\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos(useCallback((data) => data.length, []))\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"code","value":"// extracted to a stable function reference\nconst selectTodoCount = (data) => data.length\n\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos(selectTodoCount)\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further Reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an in-depth guide about these topics, read "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/preact-query-render-optimizations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query Render Optimizations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from\nthe TkDodo. To learn how to best optimize the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" option, read "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/preact-query-selectors-supercharged","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query Selectors, Supercharged"}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"structural-sharing","text":"structural sharing","level":2},{"id":"referential-identity","text":"referential identity","level":3},{"id":"tracked-properties","text":"tracked properties","level":2},{"id":"select","text":"select","level":2},{"id":"memoization","text":"memoization","level":3},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further Reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPreact Query applies a couple of optimizations automatically to ensure that your components only re-render when they actually need to. This is done by the following means:\n\n## structural sharing\n\nPreact Query uses a technique called \"structural sharing\" to ensure that as many references as possible will be kept intact between re-renders. If data is fetched over the network, usually, you'll get a completely new reference by json parsing the response. However, Preact Query will keep the original reference if _nothing_ changed in the data. If a subset changed, Preact Query will keep the unchanged parts and only replace the changed parts.\n\n> Note: This optimization only works if the `queryFn` returns JSON compatible data. You can turn it off by setting `structuralSharing: false` globally or on a per-query basis, or you can implement your own structural sharing by passing a function to it.\n\n### referential identity\n\nThe top level object returned from `useQuery`, `useInfiniteQuery`, `useMutation` and the Array returned from `useQueries` is **not referentially stable**. It will be a new reference on every render. However, the `data` properties returned from these hooks will be as stable as possible.\n\n## tracked properties\n\nPreact Query will only trigger a re-render if one of the properties returned from `useQuery` is actually \"used\". This is done by using [Proxy object](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Proxy). This avoids a lot of unnecessary re-renders, e.g. because properties like `isFetching` or `isStale` might change often, but are not used in the component.\n\nYou can customize this feature by setting `notifyOnChangeProps` manually globally or on a per-query basis. If you want to turn that feature off, you can set `notifyOnChangeProps: 'all'`.\n\n> Note: The get trap of a proxy is invoked by accessing a property, either via destructuring or by accessing it directly. If you use object rest destructuring, you will disable this optimization. We have a [lint rule](../../../eslint/no-rest-destructuring.md) to guard against this pitfall.\n\n## select\n\nYou can use the `select` option to select a subset of the data that your component should subscribe to. This is useful for highly optimized data transformations or to avoid unnecessary re-renders.\n\n```js\nexport const useTodos = (select) => {\n  return useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\n    select,\n  })\n}\n\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos((data) => data.length)\n}\n```\n\nA component using the `useTodoCount` custom hook will only re-render if the length of the todos changes. It will **not** re-render if e.g. the name of a todo changed.\n\n> Note: `select` operates on successfully cached data and is not the appropriate place to throw errors. The source of truth for errors is the `queryFn`, and a `select` function that returns an error results in `data` being `undefined` and `isSuccess` being `true`. We recommend handling errors in the `queryFn` if you wish to have a query fail on incorrect data, or outside of the query hook if you have an error case not related to caching.\n\n### memoization\n\nThe `select` function will only re-run if:\n\n- the `select` function itself changed referentially\n- `data` changed\n\nThis means that an inlined `select` function, as shown above, will run on every render. To avoid this, you can wrap the `select` function in `useCallback`, or extract it to a stable function reference if it doesn't have any dependencies:\n\n```js\n// wrapped in useCallback\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos(useCallback((data) => data.length, []))\n}\n```\n\n```js\n// extracted to a stable function reference\nconst selectTodoCount = (data) => data.length\n\nexport const useTodoCount = () => {\n  return useTodos(selectTodoCount)\n}\n```\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor an in-depth guide about these topics, read [Preact Query Render Optimizations](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/preact-query-render-optimizations) from\nthe TkDodo. To learn how to best optimize the `select` option, read [Preact Query Selectors, Supercharged](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/preact-query-selectors-supercharged)\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: render-optimizations\ntitle: Render Optimizations\nref: docs/framework/react/guides/render-optimizations.md\nreplace: { 'react-query': 'preact-query', 'React': 'Preact' }\n---\n"}
